course: everyday
lesson: 298
title: Compliments, respecting differences and disabilities
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People are different in many ways: skin colour, size, face, language, customs and how their body or brain works. Every one of them deserves to be treated kindly and fairly.
explain: When someone gives you a compliment, the polite answer is a simple "Thank you". Boasting afterwards ("Yes, I am the best at drawing in the class") spoils the good feeling for both of you.
explain: Some disabilities you can see, like using a wheelchair. Many you cannot see, like diabetes, a heart problem, autism or ADHD. If someone says they need something, such as a snack or a rest, believe them.
explain: Example: a new girl in a school in Sydney has just moved from another country and does not know that the class lines up after break. A kind classmate quietly explains how it works, so she feels welcome instead of silly.
explain: A wheelchair is part of its user's personal space. Talk to the person themselves, not to the person with them, and never lean on or push the chair without asking.
fact: When someone compliments you, say thank you and leave it there; boasting spoils the good feeling.
fact: Never tease, stare at or point at someone who looks different from you, in skin colour, size or face.
fact: Everyone deserves to be treated kindly and fairly, however they look.
fact: If a child new to the country does not know the customs, explain kindly how things work, because help makes them feel welcome.
fact: When you meet someone who uses a wheelchair, talk to them directly, not to the person with them.
fact: Do not lean on or push someone's wheelchair without asking, because it is part of their personal space.
fact: Autistic people experience the world differently; many find loud noise or sudden changes hard.
fact: Be patient and calm with an autistic classmate and respect their needs.
fact: ADHD is a difference in how the brain works that can make sitting still hard; it is not naughtiness.
fact: Many disabilities are invisible, such as diabetes or a heart problem.
fact: If someone with an invisible condition says they need a snack or a rest, believe them.
exercise: Your teacher says your story is excellent. What is the best answer? | "Thank you!" | "I know, mine is always the best." | "It is better than everyone else's." | Say nothing and look away
exercise: Why is it better not to boast after a compliment? | Boasting spoils the good feeling | Compliments must be returned at once | Teachers take compliments back if you boast | Boasting is fine if it is true
exercise: A boy in your class has a different skin colour from most of the class. What is right? | Treat him kindly and fairly like anyone else | Ask him why he looks so different every single day | Stare so you can learn about him | Leave him out until he fits in
exercise: A child in the park is much bigger than others. Your friend points and laughs. What is right? | Never tease, stare or point at someone's size | Laugh quietly behind your hand so the child does not hear | Point only if the child cannot see | Tell the child to go somewhere else
exercise: A new boy from Japan does not know that your class lines up after lunch. What do you do? | Explain kindly how it works | Laugh at his mistake | Tell the teacher he is being rude | Leave him to work it out alone
exercise: Why should we help a child new to the country with customs? | They are learning, and help makes them feel welcome | They broke the rules on purpose | It is only the teacher's job, so classmates should stay out of it | They will never learn otherwise
exercise: You meet a woman who uses a wheelchair, with her friend. Who should you talk to? | The woman herself | Her friend, who can answer for her | Nobody, it is safest to stay quiet | Whoever is standing up
exercise: You are waiting in a queue next to a man in a wheelchair. What should you not do? | Lean on his chair or push it without asking | Say hello to him | Wait your turn quietly in the queue behind him | Hold the door open if he wants
exercise: Why should you not touch someone's wheelchair without asking? | It is part of their personal space | It might roll away by itself | It is very expensive to clean | It would make the battery go flat
exercise: Your autistic classmate covers her ears when the fire alarm test is announced. What helps most? | Be patient, stay calm and respect her needs | Tell her to stop being silly | Make the noise louder so she gets used to it | Laugh to make it a joke
exercise: Which is true about autism? | Autistic people experience the world differently | Autistic people do not have feelings like everyone else | Autism is caught from other people | Autism is just bad manners
exercise: A classmate with ADHD keeps wriggling in his seat. What is kind? | Be kind and do not call him naughty | Call him naughty so he stops | Move away and ignore him forever | Tell everyone he cannot behave
exercise: What is ADHD? | A difference in how the brain works | A kind of naughtiness | An illness you can catch from other children | A sign of not trying hard
exercise: Your friend with diabetes says she needs a snack now in the middle of a game. What should you do? | Believe her and let her have it | Tell her to wait until the game ends | Say she is only pretending | Ask her to prove she is ill
test: A girl in Ohio wins the swimming race and her coach says, "Great swim!" Which reply is best? | "Thank you!" | "I am the fastest in the whole state." | "The others were so slow." | "I always win."
test: A boy has a scar across his face. What is the right thing to do? | Treat him the same as anyone else | Stare to see how it happened | Ask him about it in front of everyone | Point it out to your friends
test: A girl just moved from India to Dublin and does not know how school dinners work. What would a good classmate do? | Show her kindly how it works | Let her get it wrong to learn | Tell her she should know already | Tease her accent so she learns English
test: A waiter asks the friend of a man in a wheelchair, "What would he like?" What is wrong? | They should ask the man himself | Nothing, that is the polite way | The waiter should bring the food without asking | The friend should push the chair away
test: Which disability is usually invisible? | Diabetes | Using a wheelchair | Using a white cane | Wearing a leg brace
test: A classmate who is autistic gets upset when the timetable changes suddenly. What is true? | Sudden changes can be hard for many autistic people | She is being difficult on purpose to get her own way | She will be fine if you shout | Changes are easy for everyone
test: Your friend says, "Sam has ADHD, so he is just bad." What is true? | ADHD is a brain difference, not bad behaviour | ADHD means a child is lazy | ADHD goes away if you tell him off | Children with ADHD choose to wriggle just to annoy the teacher
test: Someone at the park looks pale and says they have a heart problem and need to sit down. What should you do? | Believe them and help them sit and rest | Ask them to prove it | Tell them they look fine | Keep playing and ignore it, they'll be fine in a bit
test: teacher said ur great at drawing! what do i say | Say thank you and leave it there | Say you're the best in the school | Say no you're not, you're rubbish | Ask her why she didn't say it before
test: kid in a wheelchair, should i talk to his helper instead | No, talk to him directly | Yes, the helper speaks for him | Yes, and speak extra slowly | Only talk to him if he talks first
test: my mate reckons kids with adhd are just naughty | No, it's a brain difference that makes sitting still hard | Yes, they could sit still if they really tried a bit harder | Yes, it goes away if they're told off | No, it only happens to grown-ups
test: girl in class needs a snack in maths cause of diabetes, thats not fair | Believe her, some conditions you can't see | It's unfair, she should wait like everyone | She's making it up for a snack | Everyone should get a snack then
explain: A girl in London was stared at and laughed at for her birthmark every day; she started to hide from other children and dreaded school.
explain: A boy in Texas pushed a classmate's wheelchair without asking as a joke; it rolled down a slope and she fell out and hurt her wrist.
fact: Teasing about how someone looks can make them anxious and lonely for years.
fact: A wheelchair is part of a person's body space, and pushing it without asking can make them fall.
test: new kid's got a weird accent, can we copy it for laughs | No, mocking hurts; be friendly | Yes, it's funny | Yes, if he doesn't hear you doing it | Only once
test: can i push jacks wheelchair fast down the ramp | Only if he asks; it can tip and hurt him | Yes, he'd love it | Yes, if you hold on | Yes, ramps are made for going fast
test: autistic boy covers his ears at assembly | Be calm and kind; loud noise can hurt him | Pull his hands away | Laugh at him | Shout louder so he hears you better
test: did u hear a boy kept pushing a kid's wheelchair for a laugh and "it tipped and she broke her wrist". lesson? | Never push or lean on a wheelchair without asking | Pushing is fine if you go slowly and only do it for a laugh | Wheelchairs can't tip over | Only adults can push them
test: my aunt says the kid with diabetes "faked needing a snack to get out of maths". what's likely | It's probably a rumour; believe someone who says they need a snack | It's true, they always fake it | Diabetes isn't real, so the snack was a trick to skip maths | Snacks are never needed in class
test: ppl say the autistic boy "is just being weird on purpose" when he covers his ears. spread it? | No, many autistic people find loud noise hard; don't spread it | Yes, he's showing off | Yes, and make loud noises near him to test if he's faking | Yes, but only to your friends
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner of addressing a person who uses a wheelchair? | One speaks to the person directly | One speaks only to their companion | One speaks loudly and slowly | One avoids speaking to them altogether
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of ADHD. | A difference in how the brain works that can make sitting still hard | A form of deliberate naughtiness | An illness caught from others | A condition caused by eating too much sugar and too many sweets
exam: In respect of disabilities, select the response consistent with the established facts. | Many disabilities, such as diabetes, are invisible | All disabilities are clearly visible | Invisible conditions are invariably imaginary or greatly exaggerated | Only wheelchair users have disabilities
exam: State the appropriate response upon receiving a compliment. | To say thank you and leave it there | To boast of one's further achievements | To insist that it is untrue | To demand a further compliment
